# DAVIS-complete

DAVIS-complete includes the [DAVIS dataset](https://www.nature.com/articles/nbt.1990) with 4,032 kinase–ligand pairs that capture substitutions, insertions, deletions, and phosphorylation events. This repository houses data preparation utilities, docking-free and docking-based training pipelines, and analysis scripts for benchmarking binding affinity prediction under multiple generalization regimes.



## Environment Setup

The reference experiments use Python 3.9.18 and CUDA 11.5. The snippet below reproduces the original environment (PyTorch 1.11.0 with CUDA 11.3). Feel free to adapt package versions if you target different hardware.

```bash
conda create --name davis-complete python=3.9
conda activate davis-complete
conda install conda-forge::pymol-open-source
conda install pytorch==1.11.0 torchvision==0.12.0 torchaudio==0.11.0 cudatoolkit=11.3 -c pytorch
pip install scipy
pip install --no-index pyg_lib torch_scatter torch_sparse torch_cluster torch_spline_conv -f https://data.pyg.org/whl/torch-1.11.0+cu113.html
pip install torch_geometric
python -m pip install PyYAML "networkx[default]" biopython rdkit-pypi e3nn spyrmsd pandas biopandas
```

## Repository Layout

```
data/                          # Downloaded raw and processed DAVIS-complete assets
docs/                          # Extended documentation and design notes
experiments/
	docking_based/
		affinity/                  # GIGN-based FDA docking pipelines
		diffdock/                  # DiffDock configuration and scripts
	docking_free/
		models/docking_free_models # Baseline graph/attention DTA implementations + outputs
logs/
	pipelines/                   # Long-running pipeline execution logs
results/
	analysis/                    # Post-processed metrics summaries
	docking_based/               # Docking-based predictions and tables
	docking_free/                # Docking-free experiment artefacts (placeholder)
scripts/
	analysis/                    # Offline analysis notebooks and scripts
	data/                        # Dataset acquisition helpers
	docking/                     # Utility scripts shared by docking pipelines
	folding/                     # Protein input preparation scripts
```

The training code remains inside the original project folders (for example `experiments/docking_free/models/docking_free_models`) so that module-relative imports continue to work. Logs and artefacts produced by the pipelines now collect under `logs/` and `results/` for easier discovery.

## Data

Download the processed DAVIS-complete data released on Zenodo and unpack it beneath `data/`:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/ZhiGroup/DAVIS-complete
cd DAVIS-complete
mkdir -p data
cd data
wget https://zenodo.org/records/15391611/files/davis_complete.tar.gz?download=1 -O davis_complete.tar.gz
tar -xvzf davis_complete.tar.gz
```

Some models also expect the DGraphDTA preprocessed assets. Unpack them inside the DGraphDTA directory:

```bash
cd ../experiments/docking_free/models/docking_free_models/DGraphDTA
wget https://zenodo.org/records/15391611/files/dgraphdta_data.tar.gz?download=1 -O dgraphdta_data.tar.gz
tar -xvzf dgraphdta_data.tar.gz
mv dgraphdta_data data
```

## Running Experiments

### Docking-free baselines (Graph/Molecule DTA)

```bash
cd experiments/docking_free/
python train_script_benchmark.py \
	--split_method both \
	--gpu 0 \
	--model_seeds 0 1 2 3 4 \
	--model_name MGraphDTA
```

Fine-tuning experiments live in the same folder. Replace the arguments (for example `--split_method same_mutation_different_drug`) to reproduce the different scenarios documented in the paper. Model checkpoints, logs, and CSV summaries are emitted alongside the scripts and can be archived to `results/docking_free/` if desired.

### Docking-based FDA (GIGN) baseline

```bash
cd experiments/docking_based/affinity/GIGN
python train_GIGN_benchmark_davis_complete_ensemble.py \
	--split_method both \
	--gpu 0 \
	--seeds 0 1 2 3 4
```

Use the fine-tuning entry point (`train_script_GIGN_benchmark_davis_complete_ensemble_fine_tuning.py`) for the same-/cross-modification generalization studies. Outputs are now collated under `experiments/docking_based/affinity/GIGN/save` and the summary CSVs should be moved or symlinked into `results/docking_based/` for long-term storage.

### DiffDock integration

`experiments/docking_based/diffdock` contains the original DiffDock-based workflows. Follow the README within that folder (if present) or mirror the commands above after sourcing the DiffDock environment.

## Analysis

Offline analysis utilities reside in `scripts/analysis/`. For example, the affinity alternation study can be rerun via:

```bash
python scripts/analysis/affinity_alternation.py
```

The script reads NPZ outputs from `experiments/docking_based` and `experiments/docking_free` (configurable through environment variables) and records summary statistics in `results/analysis/affinity_alternation_summary.txt`.

## Utilities

- `scripts/data/` contains helpers for downloading augmented, fine-tuning, and wild-type mutation datasets.
- `scripts/docking/` provides small utilities used across docking pipelines.
- `scripts/folding/` includes protein input preparation for folding experiments.

Additional onboarding notes, diagrams, or extended documentation can be placed in the `docs/` directory as the project evolves.


